A||||26||fierosound||need help with valve lash preload - this is weird||||1||
Z||000000||fierosound||03-10-2001||09:41 PM||||Here's the details 3.4L engine, GM roller cam, GM hydraulic roller lifters, 1.6 ratio Comp cams roller tip rockers.<P>I'm using a .015 feeler gauge between the roller tip and valve to "feel" when the slack is gone.  This is less subjective than turning the pushrod with your fingers to feel when it "is set".<P>I'm doing the usual procedure - tighten 'till the slack is out, then 1 full turn for preload.  I crank the engine to check the compression reading- THERE IS NONE!<P>I loosen them off and start over with only 1/2 turn preload.  Still NO compression!<P>I do this a third time, with NO preload at all, just tightening it down 'till the feeler gauge has some "drag"  then stopping.  I NOW HAVE compression!  <P>What's happening here??  Should I adjust all the valves this way?  Any "Ace Mechanics" here with any ideas on what's going on??  Are my lifters "collapsed"?  If the oil has now "bled out" of the lifters, is there a different method required to adjust them?  I've put all the tools down 'till I get some ideas on this.  Thanks in advance...<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||daved||03-10-2001||11:09 PM||||I can't see how you can use a feeler gauge to do this...I'm sure the pushrods have ball ends, and they fit in a small cup inside the lifter. To get no compression, one of the valves must be held open (assuming cam timing is correct) so you must be going way past the preload!<BR>Why not go back to the tested and true method...spinning the pushrod between your fingers.  It might be subjective, but finger tips are extremely sensitive.  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000002||PJ||03-10-2001||11:11 PM||||I like to set the preload by measuring the deflection in the pushrod seat in the lifter. Oil bleed-down in  the lifters can make it hard to set with the twisting the pushrod method. If the intake manifolds are installed already you could set it on the slightly loose side, start it and adjust to get rid of the noise. I'd rather pull the intake and set it visually though. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierosound||03-10-2001||11:59 PM||||daved...<P>I agree it wouldn't work with ball-tip pushrods. I said I'm using the feeler between the end of the valve and roller tip end of the rocker - not the pushrod end.  <P>PJ...<P>How do you adjust the rockers on a running engine in a Fiero?  On carbed engines, it's much easier, but the Fiero FI intake makes it a real chore.  I'm interested.<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000004||tgowens||03-11-2001||01:07 AM||||I tighten the rockers just until snug and then turn 1 1/2 turns to adjust.........||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierosound||03-11-2001||01:27 AM||||Thanks Terry - I know the proper procedure.  When I do it "by the book"  the valves are being held open - too much preload - and no compression.  I don't expect that the engine will run in that state. Anything more than 1/8 turn is too much.  What would cause this?<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000006||PJ||03-11-2001||09:42 AM||||I'm assuming you could set them running but I wouldn't want to try it. Two things, does the dampner have the three slots with the widest one being the timing reference? Yeah, its dumb but the first one I did I set the preload wrong by using the wrong marks. Also are you using stock pushrods with those rockers? When changing rockers and/or ratios you almost always have to change the pushrod length for proper geometry. Might be something wierd goin' on there. When I went to 6.5's I had to deepen the guides a little bit.<P>If you're setting them with the three at a time method try setting one cylinder at a time. Find TDC with a screwdriver down the sparkplug hole. Spin the pushrod 'til you just feel a slight resistance then add your preload. Try to get it right on the first try. Don't do it over and over. BTW Crane recommends 1/2 to 3/4's of a turn preload on their lifters so I'll bet you'll be alright if you don't adjust it the full turn and a half. <p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierosound||03-11-2001||10:55 AM||||This engine has been running since rebuild.  I had alot of valvetrain noise, and am readjusting the rockers because of that.<P>My harmonic balancer has only one reference mark.<P>I have custom length pushrods because I have roller lifters/iron head setup - GM never had this combination.<BR>The guideplates where machined to accomodate the pushrods properly.<P>I'm adjusting one set of valves at a time - when intake is open - adjust exhaust, then the reverse.<P>I'm doing it right, but the results aren't what's expected.<P>The problem is, ANY PRELOAD AT ALL holds the valves open!!  <P>I would expect this result if I had solid lifters.  What causes a hydraulic lifter to "lock up"<BR>and behave as if they were solids?? That's the $64,000 question.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000008||PJ||03-11-2001||12:18 PM||||Damn, I wish I was there so I could look at it. Are the pushrods designed to work with the roller lifters? The roller lifters are a lot taller than the flat base ones. Flat tappet pushrods would be too long to use with the roller lifters.<p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||03-11-2001||01:34 PM||||Actually, assuming that the valves are being held open because there's no compression isn't necessarily accurate. Remember that there will be no compression if the intake valves aren't opening. I haven't seen any mention of whether you prelubed the motor, or that the lifters are fully pumped. When I adjust my rods, I don't spin them, I move them up and down, and feel for no endplay. Once I feel there's NO slop in the rod, I do the 1- 1 1/2 additional turn. Another thing that occurs to me, is if these roller lifters were designed for this block specifically. If not, the oiler grooves may not be lining up. Not trying to insult anyones intelligence here, just trying to think of things that haven't been mentioned.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||03-11-2001||01:41 PM||||I got a question along these lines. I talked to a guy out at GM the other day, and he told me my valve lash should be only 1 turn past no play. I relize there will be variation with other engines, but Im getting confused. My Haynes manual says 1 1/2 turns, GM tells me 1 turn, some folks here on the forum have told said 3/4 turn. Which is right?||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierosound||03-11-2001||01:44 PM||||Just to let everyone know, this engine was built last September, and has been driven 2000+ miles (runs great by the way).  The rockers were noisey (too loose) and I've taken the upper intake and valve covers off to readjust them with proper preload to stop the noise.  <BR>The lifters "act" like solids.  Why???<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000012||Seanh||03-11-2001||01:55 PM||||Also, Fierosound. Your turning the engine over with the manifolds off right? Is the lower manifold off by chance? How are your valve springs? I would assume they are new since the rebuild, but Im not sure. Are the lifters freezing up, or are they working normally? Perhaps your tappet (think thats what they call it, between the valve and rocker) clearence is off? You said the engine has ran since the rebuild, so I am sure they are oiled and lubbed properly.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierosound||03-11-2001||05:46 PM||||Seanh...<P>Yes, yes, new, what do you mean - freezing up?, no - pushrod clearances are fine.<BR>What are you getting at?||||reg||10
Z||000014||Fierowrecker||03-11-2001||06:08 PM||||Here is an idea...<BR>  Remove the rocker and see how much pressure it takes to colapse the lifter...<BR>When you depress the push rod, oil should come out if any is in the lifter...<BR>If it does not colapse, it may be bad...<BR>There is a spring that should have about 20 lbs. pressure...||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cooter||03-11-2001||06:10 PM||||What brand lifters did you use? Are they link bar type or do they use the "spider" to hold them in place? What length are the pushrods? The reason I ask is because I have wanted to put the roller cam in my 3.4 also.||||reg||1
Z||000016||fierosound||03-11-2001||07:45 PM||||Fierowrecker...<P>I have already done that.  I can't compress it at all.  I haven't checked them all, but the several that I have don't budge.  <P>Cooter...<P>There is no aftermarket roller cam kit for the V6 (I could never find one).  This is a factory roller cam block from a 1976 Pontiac Transport van.  Photos of the lifters, retaining rails and valley at my website. <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/3400engine.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/3400engine.htm</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||88formula||03-11-2001||08:06 PM||||I think it’s highly unlikely that all of the lifters are collapsed at the same time. If they were not getting an oil supply than there would be no oil coming out of the pushrod tips. I'm sure you have good oil pressure too.<P>Here is what I would do. Find out what the pre-load is supposed to be for the 3.4-liter engine with roller lifters. Since you have the top intake manifold off and valve covers I would reinstall the top intake manifold without the valve covers so that the engine runs and you have access to the rocker arms. I would loosen all the rocker arms and start the engine, the rocker arms will all be clattering indicating they are out of adjustment. Turn the nut on each rocker arm just until the clattering stops and then turn it an additional blank turn to pre-load the lifter. This is the procedure that works for me. I know it’s messy but it works like a charm. If you can’t find what the pre-load should be than I would start with  1/4-1/2 a turn. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||daved||03-11-2001||08:38 PM||||Hey 88,  I tried to do that once (but on a 396).  It's VERY messy....hot oil srays everywhere from the top of the rockers...just so you know what to expect, Dave||||reg||1
Z||000019||Cplensdorf||03-11-2001||09:01 PM||||There are oil diverter 'clips' that you can purchase that hang on the rockers and deflect the oil down onto the head when you have the covers off.  They are cheap and save on the mess....until one flies off while it is running (from experience)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||tgowens||03-11-2001||09:20 PM||||if those lifters have been sitting with no lubrication they may freeze up -- if there is no oil flow into them when the engine cranks they will never fill........we have replaced a lot of lifters that were in that condition with new ones. Tried soaking the old ones and they still remained solid.........||||reg||1
Z||000021||fierosound||03-11-2001||09:21 PM||||88...<P>The engine is out of the car right now - having transmission repaired.  I had hoped to "fix" all of this so I wouldn't have to work on anything once engine is back in the car.  That option will be considered however.  Thanks...<P>As per Terry's comments,  I think the safest bet at this time is to get a new set of lifters.<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000022||PJ||03-11-2001||10:52 PM||||I have a set of roller lifters here that came out of a warrantied motor with 1,000 miles on it (scored piston). I have no use for them, so make me an offer if you think you can use them. I'll let them go cheap. I'm sure GM wants an arm and a leg for new ones.<P>PJ<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||862M4inCA||03-12-2001||12:20 AM||||hrm....replacing "bad" lifters with a set of used (even slightly) doesn't sound like a good idea to me. Judging from experience I think it would be best just to replace them rather than use a set that was used in another engine. Almost sounds as bad as mixing lifters up and not replacing them in the same bore you pulled them out of potentially creating a bigger mess. Just my .02||||reg||1
Z||000024||fierosound||03-13-2001||01:11 AM||||Woo-Hoo...<P>I installed a new set of roller lifters and everything went "according to the book"  when setting the preload on the valves.  I gave all the rockers a 3/4 turn preload.  I also checked the compression -  getting around 225 PSI in all cylinders on a cold engine.  <P>Thanks for all the suggestions and help...||||reg||10
Z||000025||tgowens||03-13-2001||03:06 AM||||and yet another Pennock's Forum success story!! Good to hear fierosound........I knew you could do it!!||||reg||1
Z||000026||88formula||03-13-2001||04:21 PM||||I bet that thing is really going to go now!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
